---
/**
 * The error summary, at the top of the form.
 *
 * Empty until a submission fails, and then it is the first thing focus moves
 * to — not the first invalid field. That ordering is the whole point of the
 * pattern: someone using a screen reader hears how many questions need
 * attention and what they are before being dropped into one of them, and
 * someone using a keyboard gets a jump list instead of a hunt down the page.
 *
 * role="alert" so the browser announces it the moment the script fills it in,
 * and tabindex="-1" so focus can be placed on it programmatically without
 * putting it into the tab order for everyone else.
 *
 * Red comes from the semantic error variable rather than from a palette token,
 * so a themed questionnaire recolours this without a component of its own. The
 * split is the same either way: the border takes the fill value, which only has
 * to clear 3:1 as a non-text element, and the heading takes the ink value, held
 * to the 4.5:1 small text needs against whatever ground the page has. Unthemed
 * they are the status palette's red and its deep, which is what they were.
 */
---

<div id="questionnaire-errors" role="alert" tabindex="-1" hidden class="q-band q-tone-error ww-errors">
	<p class="ww-eyebrow ww-errors-heading" data-error-heading></p>

	<ul class="ww-errors-list" data-error-list></ul>
</div>

<style>
	.ww-errors {
		margin-bottom: var(--space-lg);
	}

	.ww-errors-heading {
		color: var(--q-error-ink);
	}

	.ww-errors-list {
		display: flex;
		flex-direction: column;
		gap: var(--space-xs);
		margin: var(--space-sm) 0 0;
		padding: 0;
		list-style: none;
	}
</style>
